; `.default` on a head that resolved to something other than a matching rust PRIMITIVE aborts, at
; `RustType::default`'s validity assertion, instead of rejecting. The head here is a REFUSED prelude
; name, which makes this the closure-breach shape: `new_type` has already recorded the graceful
; refusal for `undefined` and returned its inert `Fixed(Null)` placeholder, and the `.default`
; application then panics one step later — destroying a rejection `finalize` would have reported.
; The abort is not exclusive to refused names (a SUPPORTED prelude name with no rust primitive,
; e.g. `tdate`, aborts identically), so the fix is a refusal at the `.default` application rather
; than at any existing name seam. Every cell of the class is pinned by
; `refused_name_closure_tests::KNOWN_CLOSURE_BREACH` (rule and member positions, all five refused
; names); this fixture is its outcome-category row.
x = undefined .default 1
